  "query_preview": "/MONDAY NIGHT THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 315 AM CDT Sun Aug 23 2026\n\nAn upper level trough of low pressure, will traverse south \ncentral Canada Monday afternoon into Monday night, forcing a \ncold front through the forecast area. The latest deterministic \nNAM12 and GFS solns along with the NBM ensembles indicate a \ndecent chance for showers and thunderstorms across a large \nportion of the forecast area with the best chances across the \nwest and southwest. The latest NBM ensembles indicate 60 to 70 \npercent chance of exceeding 0.10\" of precipitation Monday night \ngenerally west of a line from Gordon to Broken Bow. The NBM \nforecast for tonight has some high chance and low likely pops in\nthe eastern panhandle and southwestern Nebraska Monday night \nand this looks on track. The upper level low/trough, will track \ninto the northern Great Lakes Tuesday into Tuesday night \nallowing the ridge to amplify across the western CONUS. \nNorthwesterly flow aloft will continue into the end of the week \nwith periodic shortwaves tracking across the central plains. \nThis will lead to daily precipitation chances through the end of\nthe period. Temperatures will be coolest in the east and \nnortheast with daily highs in the lower 80s. Across the eastern \npanhandle and southwestern Nebraska, daily highs will be in the \nupper 80s to lower 90s.",
